window.__hvBdRegister("rep", {"key":"rep","sections":[{"i":0,"j":21,"loc":"327a–331d","title":"Down to the Piraeus: Cephalus on old age","summary":"Socrates tells how he and Glaucon, returning from a festival at the Piraeus, were playfully detained by Polemarchus and brought to the house of his father Cephalus. The aged Cephalus says that the troubles of old age come from character, not years, and cites Sophocles' relief at escaping the passions. Asked what good his wealth has done him, he answers that it lets a man die without having cheated anyone or owing sacrifices to gods or debts to men. Socrates asks whether justice, then, is telling the truth and paying back what one owes, and shows that returning weapons to a friend gone mad would not be just.","ideas":["Contentment in old age depends on a calm and orderly character more than on wealth or years.","Fear of punishment after death makes a man review the wrongs he has done.","Justice cannot simply be truth-telling and repaying debts, since there are cases where these would be wrong."],"terms":[{"t":"dikaiosunē (justice)","d":"The quality under enquiry; Cephalus' practice suggests it means honesty and paying what is owed."}]},{"i":22,"j":44,"loc":"331d–336a","title":"Polemarchus: helping friends and harming enemies","summary":"Cephalus leaves for his sacrifices and Polemarchus takes over the argument, citing Simonides: justice is giving each what is owed, which he interprets as good to friends and harm to enemies. Socrates' questions make justice seem useful only for keeping idle money safe, and then, since the best guard is also the best thief, an art of stealing. Polemarchus revises the view so that friends are those who really are good, but Socrates argues that harming any man makes him worse in human excellence, that is, more unjust, and the just man cannot produce injustice. They agree that the saying belongs not to a wise man but to some rich tyrant.","ideas":["Comparing justice with crafts like medicine and piloting makes it look useless in peace.","One who can best guard something can best steal it, so a skill alone cannot define justice.","Harming someone makes him worse in his own excellence, so the just man harms no one."],"terms":[{"t":"technē (art, craft)","d":"A skilled practice with its own task, used here as the model for testing what justice does."},{"t":"aretē (virtue, excellence)","d":"The quality that makes a thing good of its kind; for a human being it is taken to be justice."}]},{"i":45,"j":63,"loc":"336b–339e","title":"Thrasymachus: justice as the interest of the stronger","summary":"Thrasymachus bursts into the conversation, mocks Socrates' irony, and demands that he give an answer of his own rather than refuting others. After some sparring over payment, he states his own definition: justice is the interest of the stronger, meaning that each government makes laws for its own advantage and calls obedience to them just. Socrates gets him to admit that rulers can err and that subjects must obey all their laws. It follows that subjects are sometimes justly commanded to act against the rulers' interest, which Polemarchus declares obvious.","ideas":["Thrasymachus treats the laws of every regime as tools of the ruling power's advantage.","If rulers can mistake their interest and must still be obeyed, justice can harm the stronger.","Thrasymachus charges that Socrates only questions and never answers."],"terms":[{"t":"eirōneia (irony)","d":"Thrasymachus' word for Socrates' feigned ignorance, used as a way of avoiding giving an answer."},{"t":"to tou kreittonos sumpheron (interest of the stronger)","d":"Thrasymachus' definition of justice: what benefits whoever holds power."}]},{"i":64,"j":78,"loc":"340a–342e","title":"The ruler in the strict sense serves his subjects","summary":"Cleitophon suggests Thrasymachus meant what the stronger believes to be his interest, but Thrasymachus refuses and instead argues that a ruler, strictly speaking, never errs, just as a physician does not err insofar as he is a physician. Socrates accepts the strict sense and turns it against him: the true physician heals the sick rather than making money, and the true pilot commands sailors. Every art, being complete in itself, seeks the good of its subject, not its own. So no ruler, as ruler, seeks his own advantage, but that of those he rules, and Thrasymachus' definition appears overturned.","ideas":["A practitioner in the strict sense makes no mistakes insofar as he practises his art.","Arts lack nothing themselves and so look to the good of what they work on.","Rule, as an art, aims at the advantage of the ruled."],"terms":[{"t":"akribēs logos (strict account)","d":"The precise sense of a craft-name, in which the craftsman is considered only as exercising his skill."}]},{"i":79,"j":104,"loc":"343a–348a","title":"The shepherd, the tyrant and the wages of rule","summary":"Thrasymachus replies that Socrates cannot tell the shepherd from the sheep: rulers fatten their subjects for their own profit, justice is another's good, and the unjust man always comes out ahead, most of all the tyrant, who is called blessed. Socrates insists that the true shepherd, like the true ruler, cares only for his flock, and that pay comes from a separate art of wage-earning. This is why rulers must be paid in money, honour, or a penalty; the good rule only to avoid being ruled by worse men. Socrates then proposes, with Glaucon, to test Thrasymachus' more serious claim that the unjust life is better.","ideas":["Thrasymachus claims justice benefits others while injustice on a grand scale brings mastery and happiness.","The wage-earning art is distinct from the art each craftsman practises.","Good men take office reluctantly, chiefly to avoid being ruled by worse men."],"terms":[{"t":"allotrion agathon (another's good)","d":"Thrasymachus' description of justice as benefiting the ruler and harming the just person who practises it."},{"t":"misthōtikē (art of wage-earning)","d":"Socrates' name for the separate art by which practitioners of other arts get paid."}]},{"i":105,"j":117,"loc":"348b–350d","title":"Is injustice wisdom? The argument from outdoing","summary":"Thrasymachus declares that injustice is good counsel and discretion, while justice is noble simplicity, placing injustice among wisdom and virtue. Socrates asks whom each man tries to outdo: the just man seeks to get more than the unjust but not the just, while the unjust seeks more than everyone. Comparing musicians and physicians, he shows that the knowing person does not try to outdo others who know, whereas the ignorant tries to outdo both. So the just man resembles the wise and good and the unjust the ignorant and bad. Thrasymachus agrees with great reluctance and, sweating on a hot day, blushes.","ideas":["Thrasymachus reverses the usual praise, calling injustice good judgement.","Those with knowledge do not compete with others who know; the ignorant compete with everyone.","By this test the just man is like the wise, the unjust like the ignorant."],"terms":[{"t":"pleonexia (outdoing, grasping for more)","d":"The desire to have or do more than others, used to compare the just and unjust man."}]},{"i":118,"j":134,"loc":"350e–354a","title":"Injustice as weakness; the soul's proper work","summary":"Thrasymachus now answers only to please the company. Socrates argues that even a band of thieves can act together only if they are not unjust to one another, so injustice produces faction and weakness in a city, a group, or a single soul at odds with itself. He then argues that everything has a function and an excellence by which it performs it; the soul's work includes ruling, deliberating and living, and justice is its excellence, so the just man lives well and is happy. Thrasymachus concedes, but Socrates closes by admitting that, having never settled what justice is, he knows nothing.","ideas":["Injustice breeds hatred and faction and so makes joint action impossible.","Each thing does its work well through its own excellence.","Without knowing what justice is, one cannot know whether it makes one happy."],"terms":[{"t":"ergon (function, work)","d":"What a thing alone does, or does best, such as seeing for the eye."},{"t":"aretē (excellence)","d":"The condition by which a thing performs its function well; justice is that of the soul."}]},{"i":135,"j":164,"loc":"354b–362c","title":"Glaucon's challenge and the Ring of Gyges","summary":"Glaucon, dissatisfied, asks Socrates to show that justice is good in itself, and Socrates places it among goods valued both for themselves and their results. Glaucon then restates the popular view: justice is a compact among the weak, a compromise between doing wrong with impunity and suffering it. He tells the story of Gyges, whose ring of invisibility led him to seize a kingdom, to show that anyone would be unjust if he could act unseen. Finally he sets up a perfectly unjust man with a reputation for justice beside a perfectly just man thought unjust and tortured, and asks which is happier.","ideas":["Goods may be valued for themselves, for their consequences, or for both.","On the common view justice is a contract made from weakness, not a good in itself.","To judge justice fairly, its reputation must be stripped away from it."],"terms":[{"t":"Ring of Gyges","d":"A ring that makes its wearer invisible, imagined to test whether anyone is just willingly."},{"t":"sunthēkē (compact)","d":"The agreement among people neither to do nor suffer injustice, said to be the origin of law."}]},{"i":165,"j":191,"loc":"362d–367e","title":"Adeimantus on how justice is praised","summary":"Adeimantus adds that even those who praise justice, parents, poets and teachers, praise only its rewards and reputation, promising divine blessings to the just. Poets and itinerant priests also say virtue is hard and vice easy, and that the gods can be bought off with sacrifices and rites. A clever young man will conclude that he should cultivate a mere appearance of justice and use rites to escape punishment. Adeimantus therefore asks Socrates to set aside reputation and show what justice and injustice do, in themselves, to the soul that has them.","ideas":["Conventional praise of justice values its rewards and reputation, not justice itself.","Belief that the gods can be bribed undermines any reason to be just.","The real question is the effect of justice and injustice within the soul."],"terms":[{"t":"doxa (reputation, seeming)","d":"The appearance of being just, which the brothers ask Socrates to exclude from his defence."}]},{"i":192,"j":214,"loc":"368a–372c","title":"The city as large letters; the first city","summary":"Socrates praises the brothers and agrees to defend justice. He proposes to look for it first in the city, like reading large letters before small ones, and then in the individual. A city arises because no one is self-sufficient; farmer, builder, weaver and shoemaker each do one job suited to their nature. As needs grow, the city adds toolmakers, herdsmen, merchants, sailors, a market, retailers and hired labourers. Asked where justice lies in this city, Adeimantus suggests it lies in the citizens' dealings with one another, and Socrates describes their simple, peaceful life.","ideas":["Justice in the larger city should be easier to see than in one person.","Cities arise from mutual need and exchange.","Work is best done when each person does the one task natural to him."],"terms":[{"t":"polis (city, state)","d":"The community of mutually dependent people, used as a larger image of the soul."}]},{"i":215,"j":234,"loc":"372d–376c","title":"The luxurious city, war, and the guardian's nature","summary":"Glaucon calls the simple city a city of pigs and asks for comforts, so Socrates turns to a feverish, luxurious city full of new trades, though he calls the first city the healthy one. Luxury requires more land and leads to war, which in turn needs a professional army, since one person should practise one art. The guardians must be spirited yet gentle to their own, a combination Socrates first thinks impossible. The well-bred dog, fierce to strangers and gentle to acquaintances, shows it is possible, and it shows a kind of love of learning, so the guardian must combine spirit with philosophy.","ideas":["Wanting more than necessities is traced as the source of war.","Soldiering is an art requiring a class devoted to it.","Guardians, like good watchdogs, must be fierce to enemies and gentle to friends."],"terms":[{"t":"thumos (spirit)","d":"The spirited, fearless element that makes a guardian brave."},{"t":"phulax (guardian)","d":"A member of the class charged with defending the city."}]},{"i":235,"j":253,"loc":"376d–380b","title":"Education through stories; God causes only good","summary":"Socrates turns to the guardians' education, music for the soul and gymnastic for the body, beginning with the stories told to children. Because the young absorb what they hear, the founders must supervise storytellers and reject tales such as Hesiod's account of Uranus and Cronus or the battles of the gods. Socrates sets out the first pattern for speaking of the gods: God is truly good, and the good cannot cause harm. So God is the cause of good things only, and poets may not say he brings evils on people; any punishment must be presented as benefiting those punished.","ideas":["Early stories shape character, so poets' tales about the gods must be censored.","The founders set patterns for poets but do not write the poems.","Since God is good, God is the cause of good things, not of all things."],"terms":[{"t":"mousikē (music)","d":"Education in the arts of the Muses, including stories and poetry, aimed at the soul."},{"t":"tupoi peri theologias (patterns of theology)","d":"The outlines to which poets' accounts of the gods must conform."}]},{"i":254,"j":268,"loc":"380c–383b","title":"God neither changes nor deceives","summary":"Socrates proposes a second pattern: the gods do not change form. What is best is least altered by outside forces, and a perfect god would not willingly change himself for the worse, so tales of gods in disguise must go. Nor would a god deceive: the true lie, ignorance in the soul about what matters most, is hated by gods and men, while a lie in words is only useful for dealing with enemies, mad friends, or unknown antiquity. None of these motives applies to God. Poems such as Zeus' lying dream to Agamemnon are therefore rejected, and Adeimantus accepts both patterns as laws.","ideas":["What is best is least subject to change from within or without.","Falsehood in the soul about the highest things is the truest and most hated lie.","Spoken lies may be useful to humans as a remedy, but never to God."],"terms":[{"t":"to hōs alēthōs pseudos (the true lie)","d":"Being deceived in one's soul about the most important matters, as opposed to a lie in words."}]},{"i":269,"j":287,"loc":"383c–389c","title":"Fear of death, lamentation, laughter and lying","summary":"To make the guardians brave, Socrates would strike out Homer's grim descriptions of Hades and the dread names of the underworld, since anyone who fears death will prefer slavery to death in battle. Lamentations by famous men such as Achilles and Priam, and especially by gods, must go too, because the good man is self-sufficient and does not find a comrade's death terrible. Nor should worthy men or gods be shown overcome by laughter. Lying is allowed only to the rulers, like medicine to physicians, and a private citizen who lies to them is to be punished.","ideas":["Courage requires that the world below not be pictured as terrible.","The good man is least dependent on others and so least given to grief.","Only rulers may use falsehood, and only for the city's good."],"terms":[{"t":"andreia (courage)","d":"The virtue that the censorship of tales about death is meant to foster."}]},{"i":288,"j":302,"loc":"389d–392c","title":"Temperance, money and the misdeeds of heroes","summary":"Socrates turns to temperance, defined as obedience to rulers and self-control in pleasures, and approves passages showing discipline and endurance. He rejects insolence to commanders, praise of feasting, the tale of Zeus overcome by desire for Hera, and stories of Achilles' greed, insolence to gods and cruelty to Hector's body. Heroes who are sons of gods must not be shown doing terrible deeds, since young people will excuse their own vices. Tales about men, such as that the unjust often prosper, must wait until they have found out what justice is.","ideas":["Stories must model obedience, endurance and self-control.","Poets may not make heroes both children of gods and wrongdoers.","Rules for stories about men depend on first finding what justice is."],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosunē (temperance)","d":"Obedience to rulers and mastery over one's own pleasures."}]},{"i":303,"j":331,"loc":"392d–398b","title":"Narration and imitation: the poet's style","summary":"Having dealt with content, Socrates turns to style, distinguishing simple narration, imitation in which the poet speaks as a character, and a mix of the two, using the opening of the Iliad as his example. Since each person does one thing well, guardians should imitate, if at all, only good characters, because imitation practised from youth becomes habit and second nature. The good man narrates mostly in his own voice, while the bad will imitate anything, even animal noises and thunder. The city will admit only the austere imitator of the virtuous; the versatile mimic is honoured, crowned, and sent away to another city.","ideas":["Poetry is narration, imitation, or a mixture of the two.","Repeated imitation forms the character of the imitator.","The one-person-one-task principle limits what guardians may imitate."],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"Speaking or acting in the person of another, so as to resemble them."},{"t":"diēgēsis (narration)","d":"Telling events in the poet's own voice."}]},{"i":332,"j":357,"loc":"398c–403c","title":"Modes, rhythms, grace, and the love of beauty","summary":"With Glaucon, Socrates turns to song: melody and rhythm must follow the words. Mournful and slack modes such as the Lydian and Ionian are banned, leaving the Dorian and Phrygian for courage and temperance, together with only the lyre and cithara, and a pipe for shepherds. Rhythms should express an orderly, brave life, with the details left to Damon. Grace and good rhythm follow good character, so all the crafts should be supervised, letting the young grow up among beautiful things. Music's aim is the love of beauty, and lovers must avoid mad and intemperate pleasure.","ideas":["Melody and rhythm should follow words, and words follow character.","Musical training sinks deep into the soul, so a well-trained person loves the fine before he can reason.","The proper end of music is the love of the beautiful."],"terms":[{"t":"harmonia (mode)","d":"A musical scale or mode, each held to express a particular kind of character."},{"t":"euschēmosunē (grace)","d":"Gracefulness of form or movement, which follows from good rhythm and character."}]},{"i":358,"j":365,"loc":"403d–405a","title":"Simple gymnastic and the soldier's diet","summary":"Socrates turns to gymnastic, proposing that a good soul improves the body, not the reverse, so only general outlines are needed. Guardians must avoid drunkenness and should not follow the sleepy regimen of professional athletes, which breaks down at any change. Like wakeful dogs on campaign, they need a simple training, matching simple music, and Homer's heroes eat plain roasted meat without fish or sauces. Syracusan feasts, Corinthian mistresses and Attic pastries are excluded, since complexity in music breeds license and in diet breeds disease.","ideas":["Care for the soul comes first and the body's care follows.","Military training must be simple and robust, not specialized.","Simplicity produces temperance in the soul and health in the body."],"terms":[{"t":"gumnastikē (gymnastic)","d":"Physical training and regimen, here designed for soldiers rather than athletes."}]},{"i":366,"j":391,"loc":"405b–410b","title":"Doctors, lawsuits, Asclepius and the good judge","summary":"Socrates calls it a sign of bad education when a city needs many lawyers and doctors, and condemns habitual litigants and those who make themselves ill through idleness. He blames Herodicus for inventing a lingering medicine that tends chronic illness, while Asclepius, he says, treated only the basically healthy, since everyone in a well-ordered city has work to do. Glaucon asks whether the best doctors and judges are those with the widest experience. Socrates answers that doctors may well have been ill themselves, but judges must learn evil by observing others when old, not from their own souls. The city will treat sound natures and leave the incurable to die or put them to death.","ideas":["Constant need for courts and doctors shows a failure of education.","Endlessly nursing the body hinders work, virtue and thought.","A good judge knows vice by long observation, not by having practised it."],"terms":[{"t":"Asclepius","d":"The god of healing, presented here as a statesman who healed only those able to live a working life."}]},{"i":392,"j":401,"loc":"410c–412b","title":"Music and gymnastic both train the soul","summary":"Socrates argues that music and gymnastic are not meant one for the soul and one for the body, but both chiefly for the soul. Gymnastic alone makes men harsh and savage, music alone makes them soft, because one develops the spirited element and the other the philosophic. Too much music melts a man's spirit and leaves him either feeble or irritable; too much exercise without the Muses leaves his mind dull and makes him a hater of argument who acts by force. The true musician is one who blends the two in the soul like tuned strings, and the city will always need such a person to oversee it.","ideas":["Spirit and love of wisdom must be tuned together like the strings of an instrument.","One-sided training produces either brutality or softness.","Further details of education follow from these general principles."],"terms":[{"t":"to philosophon (the philosophic element)","d":"The gentle, learning-loving part of human nature that music trains."}]},{"i":402,"j":411,"loc":"412c–414b","title":"Choosing the rulers by trial","summary":"Socrates asks who should rule: the elder over the younger, and the best guardians, meaning those who care most for the city because they think its interests are their own. They must be watched from childhood to see whether they hold to this conviction, since beliefs can be lost through theft (persuasion or forgetting), force (pain), or enchantment (pleasure or fear). Candidates are to be tested with tasks, toils and trials of fear and pleasure, like gold in the fire. Those who pass are to be rulers, the full guardians, while the younger men are now renamed auxiliaries.","ideas":["A person cares most for what he believes shares his own interests.","True beliefs are lost only unwillingly, by theft, force or enchantment.","Rulers are chosen by lifelong testing of their devotion to the city."],"terms":[{"t":"epikouroi (auxiliaries)","d":"The younger guardians who support and enforce the rulers' decisions."}]},{"i":412,"j":425,"loc":"414c–417a","title":"The noble lie and the guardians' way of life","summary":"Hesitating, Socrates proposes one grand falsehood, a Phoenician tale: the citizens were formed inside the earth, so they are brothers and must defend their land as mother. God mixed gold into rulers, silver into auxiliaries and bronze and iron into farmers and craftsmen, and children must be moved up or down according to their metal. Glaucon doubts the first generation could believe it, but thinks later ones might. The guardians then encamp where they can guard the city, live in common without private property, and touch no gold or silver, lest they turn from watchdogs into wolves and tyrants.","ideas":["A founding myth is meant to make citizens care for the city and one another.","Rank is to follow the nature of the soul, not birth.","Guardians must have no private property, so that they do not prey on those they guard."],"terms":[{"t":"gennaion pseudos (noble lie)","d":"The useful fiction of earth-born citizens with souls of different metals."},{"t":"myth of the metals","d":"The tale assigning gold, silver, or bronze and iron to each class."}]},{"i":426,"j":448,"loc":"417b–423e","title":"Are the guardians happy? Wealth, poverty and unity","summary":"Adeimantus objects that the guardians, owning nothing and merely fed, are made miserable though the city belongs to them. Socrates replies that the city was founded for the happiness of the whole, not of one class, as a statue is made beautiful by proportion rather than by gilding the eyes. He then names wealth and poverty as twin causes of decay in the arts, and argues that a lean, trained city can defeat rich enemies, since other cities are really many cities, split between rich and poor. The city should grow only as far as unity allows, and children are to be moved up or down by nature.","ideas":["The aim is the happiness of the whole city, each class receiving what its nature allows","Wealth breeds idleness and luxury, poverty meanness; both must be kept out","Ordinary cities are divided into a city of the rich and a city of the poor","The right size of a city is set by its unity and self-sufficiency"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkēs (self-sufficing)","d":"The city should be neither large nor small but one and sufficient for itself."}]},{"i":449,"j":468,"loc":"424a–427e","title":"Education as the one great thing","summary":"Socrates says the many rules laid down are trifles beside the one sufficient thing, education and nurture, from which matters such as marriage and the common possession of women and children will follow. Music and gymnastic must be kept free of innovation, for, as Damon says, changes in musical modes bring changes in the laws; Adeimantus adds that lawlessness creeps in through amusement until it overturns everything. Well-educated citizens will work out minor customs and the business of markets and contracts for themselves, and legislators who keep patching such details are like invalids chasing cures, or like cutting at a hydra. The greatest matters, temples, sacrifices and rites for the dead, are left to Apollo of Delphi.","ideas":["A well-started State gathers force like a wheel through good nurture","Musical innovation is dangerous because lawlessness enters through play","Detailed legislation is useless in bad States and unnecessary in good ones","Religious ordinances are referred to the Delphic god"],"terms":[{"t":"mousikē (music)","d":"Education in poetry and song, whose forms the rulers must keep unchanged."}]},{"i":469,"j":482,"loc":"428a–430d","title":"Finding wisdom and courage in the city","summary":"Socrates, with Glaucon now answering, sets out to find justice in the completed city. Assuming the good city is wise, courageous, temperate and just, he proposes to find three virtues so that justice will be the remainder. The city is wise not through carpenters' or farmers' skills but through the knowledge of the small ruling class that counsels for the whole. It is courageous through its soldiers, who preserve under pleasure, pain, desire and fear the lawful opinion about what is to be feared, fixed in them like a fast dye.","ideas":["The four virtues are sought by elimination, justice being the last","Wisdom resides in the smallest class, the perfect guardians","Courage is the preservation of right opinion about dangers","Education prepares the soul to take the laws like dye on prepared wool"],"terms":[{"t":"sophia (wisdom)","d":"Good counsel about the city as a whole, found in its rulers."},{"t":"andreia (courage)","d":"The saving, under all circumstances, of the lawful opinion about what is truly to be feared."}]},{"i":483,"j":503,"loc":"430e–434e","title":"Temperance and the discovery of justice","summary":"At Glaucon's request temperance is examined before justice. Socrates explains it as a kind of harmony: the better part ruling the worse, so that the city is master of itself, and an agreement among rulers and ruled about who should rule, running through all classes like notes of a scale. Then, after a mock hunt through a dark wood, he declares that justice was at their feet from the start: it is the founding principle that each does his own work and is not a busybody. Exchanges among craftsmen do little harm, but meddling between the three classes is the greatest harm to the city and is injustice.","ideas":["Self-mastery means the rule of the better part over the worse","Temperance, unlike wisdom and courage, extends through the whole city","Justice is each class doing its own business, the condition of the other virtues","Meddling among the three classes ruins the city and is injustice"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosynē (temperance)","d":"The agreement of superior and inferior about who should rule, a harmony across all classes."},{"t":"dikaiosynē (justice)","d":"Doing one's own work and having what is one's own, without meddling in others' functions."}]},{"i":504,"j":527,"loc":"435a–439d","title":"The soul divided: reason and appetite","summary":"Socrates proposes to test the city's account of justice on the individual, assuming the soul has the same three principles as the city, though he warns that a longer method would be needed for precision. He states that the same thing cannot act or be acted upon in opposite ways in the same part and respect at the same time, defending it against the examples of the man standing still and the spinning top. Analysing thirst as desire for drink simply, and relatives in general, he argues that when a thirsty person refuses to drink, something forbidding opposes something bidding. He concludes that the soul has a rational part and an irrational, appetitive part.","ideas":["State and soul are compared as larger and smaller instances of one form","A principle of opposites: contrary actions imply distinct parts","Thirst as such is desire for drink, not for good drink","Conflict between bidding and forbidding reveals reason distinct from appetite"],"terms":[{"t":"to logistikon (the rational part)","d":"The part of the soul with which one reasons and which can check desire."},{"t":"to epithymētikon (the appetitive part)","d":"The part with which one hungers, thirsts and desires, ally of pleasures."}]},{"i":528,"j":549,"loc":"439e–444a","title":"Spirit, the three-part soul and inner justice","summary":"Socrates tells of Leontius, who longed to look at corpses and rebuked his own eyes, to show that anger can war against desire. Spirit sides with reason against desire, is roused by perceived wrong, and differs from reason too, since children and animals have it without reason, as Homer's line about rebuking the heart shows. The individual is then wise, courageous, temperate and just in the same ways as the city, with reason ruling, spirit as its ally, and both governing appetite. Justice is shown to be an inner order of the soul, of which the outward division of labour was only a shadow, and the just man acts so as to preserve this harmony.","ideas":["Spirit is a third part, the natural auxiliary of reason","The individual's virtues mirror the city's","The just man binds the three parts together like notes of a scale","Justice concerns the inward man, not merely outward actions"],"terms":[{"t":"thymos (spirit)","d":"The passionate, angry element that fights for what it judges just and obeys reason."}]},{"i":550,"j":557,"loc":"444b–445d","title":"Injustice as disease; the forms of vice","summary":"Socrates describes injustice as civil war among the parts of the soul, a part rising against the whole. Justice and injustice are to the soul what health and disease are to the body: natural order or disorder of ruling and being ruled. Glaucon now calls it ridiculous to ask whether injustice pays, since life is not worth living with a corrupted soul. Socrates nonetheless proposes to survey the forms of vice, saying virtue is one but vice manifold, with five forms of State and soul, the first being the one described, called monarchy or aristocracy.","ideas":["Injustice is rebellion of a part of the soul against the whole","Virtue is the health of the soul, vice its disease","Virtue is one; there are four notable forms of vice","There are five forms of State matched by five forms of soul"],"terms":[{"t":"aristokratia (aristocracy)","d":"The rule of the best, by one or many, which is the constitution just described."}]},{"i":558,"j":569,"loc":"445e–451a","title":"Socrates is stopped: women and children","summary":"As Socrates begins on the four bad forms of State, Polemarchus whispers to Adeimantus, and they refuse to let him pass over the remark that friends will have women and children in common. Glaucon and Thrasymachus join in demanding a full account of how such a community will work. Socrates protests that the subject raises doubts both about possibility and about desirability, and that speaking as a hesitant enquirer risks misleading friends about justice and beauty. Glaucon laughingly acquits him in advance, and Socrates agrees to speak.","ideas":["The friends insist on a fuller account of family arrangements","Doubts concern both possibility and benefit","Socrates fears deceiving friends more than being laughed at"],"terms":[]},{"i":570,"j":588,"loc":"451b–454e","title":"The first wave: women guardians","summary":"Socrates proposes that women be educated like the men, in music, gymnastic and war, as female watchdogs share the work of males. He anticipates ridicule at women exercising naked, recalling that male nakedness in gymnasia once seemed ridiculous, and holds that only folly and vice deserve laughter. He then argues against himself: they agreed that different natures should have different work, and men and women differ. He diagnoses this as mere verbal contention, which fails to ask what kind of difference matters, as a difference between bald and hairy men does not bar either from cobbling.","ideas":["Women and men guardians should share the same nurture and duties","Custom, not reason, makes the proposal look ridiculous","The objection rests on contention over words, not division of kinds","Only differences relevant to a pursuit justify different pursuits"],"terms":[{"t":"antilogikē (art of contradiction)","d":"Disputing over verbal oppositions without defining and dividing the subject."}]},{"i":589,"j":601,"loc":"455a–457c","title":"Same natures, same pursuits for women","summary":"Socrates argues that bearing versus begetting children is no difference relevant to education or to governing. Natural gifts, such as quickness to learn, are spread across both sexes, though he and Glaucon hold that women are on the whole weaker. Some women are fit to be guardians and should be chosen as colleagues of male guardians and receive the same training. The law is therefore possible and in accordance with nature, and also most beneficial, since it makes the best men and women; the women guardians should share in war, with lighter tasks.","ideas":["No civic function belongs to a woman as woman or a man as man","Guardian qualities are found in both sexes, differing in strength","The proposal is both possible and best","The useful is the noble and the hurtful the base"],"terms":[{"t":"physis (nature)","d":"A person's natural aptitudes, which decide the work and education fitting for them."}]},{"i":602,"j":624,"loc":"457d–462a","title":"The second wave: community of wives and children","summary":"Socrates announces a greater wave: guardians' wives and children are to be common, and no parent will know his own child. Glaucon demands proof of both utility and possibility, and Socrates defers possibility. Drawing on the breeding of dogs and birds, he proposes that the best be mated with the best as often as possible, using festivals and rigged lots, a medicinal falsehood known only to the rulers; brave young men are to be favoured. Children are reared in common nurseries, parents breed only within set ages, and kinship names are assigned by date of birth.","ideas":["Marriage is to be made sacred and as beneficial as possible","The rulers use secret lots and falsehood to regulate unions","No mother or father is to recognise their own child","Kinship terms are assigned by the timing of births"],"terms":[{"t":"gamoi hieroi (sacred marriages)","d":"Festival unions arranged by the rulers for the best possible offspring."},{"t":"pharmakon (medicine)","d":"The rulers' lies, treated as a remedy used for the subjects' good."}]},{"i":625,"j":647,"loc":"462b–466d","title":"Unity as the greatest good of the city","summary":"Socrates asks what the greatest good and evil of a city are, and answers unity and division. Unity exists where citizens share pleasures and pains and say mine and not mine of the same things, like a body feeling a hurt finger as a whole. In their city rulers are called saviours and the people maintainers, and every guardian treats others as kin, so that common property and common families remove lawsuits, quarrels and the petty cares of households. The guardians' life is more blessed than that of Olympic victors, which answers Adeimantus's earlier objection, and Glaucon agrees that women share fully in the guardians' life.","ideas":["Discord is the greatest evil, unity the greatest good","The best city is like one body sharing pain and pleasure","Common families and property remove the occasions of strife","Guardians' life is happier than Olympic victors'"],"terms":[{"t":"koinōnia (community)","d":"Shared possession of goods, women and children among the guardians, producing common feeling."}]},{"i":648,"j":673,"loc":"466e–471e","title":"Conduct in war and towards Hellenes","summary":"Socrates describes how the guardians will make war: children ride out on swift horses to watch battles, under veterans, as potters' boys watch the wheel. Cowards are degraded, the captured abandoned, and the brave crowned, kissed, given more wives, and honoured with feasts; those who die gloriously are revered as of the golden race. Hellenes must not enslave Hellenes, strip the dead, dedicate kinsmen's arms, or burn houses and lay waste land. Socrates distinguishes war, against barbarians, from discord, among Hellenes who are by nature friends, and Glaucon agrees to a law against ravaging Hellenic lands.","ideas":["Children are trained by safely watching war","Courage is rewarded with honours, kisses and more offspring","War is against foreigners; strife among Hellenes is discord","Hellenes at odds should act as correctors, not destroyers"],"terms":[{"t":"stasis (discord)","d":"Internal strife among kin, such as Hellenes fighting Hellenes, as distinct from war."},{"t":"polemos (war)","d":"Hostility against those who are by nature foreign enemies."}]},{"i":674,"j":683,"loc":"472a–473e","title":"The third wave: ideal and possibility","summary":"Glaucon interrupts, granting all the benefits and demanding to know whether such a city is possible. Socrates calls this the third and greatest wave, and first reminds him that they sought justice as an ideal standard to judge by, not to prove it could exist, as a painter of a perfect man need not show such a man exists. Since deeds fall short of words, it is enough to show how a city may come nearest the pattern. He proposes to name the least change that could bring this about, and braces himself for laughter.","ideas":["Ideals of justice serve as standards for judging ourselves","An ideal is no worse for being unrealisable in full","Action always falls short of what words express","The question becomes the smallest change that would suffice"],"terms":[{"t":"paradeigma (pattern)","d":"The ideal city or just man used as a standard of comparison."}]},{"i":684,"j":697,"loc":"474a–476d","title":"Philosopher-kings and the lovers of sights","summary":"Socrates declares that cities will not cease from evil until philosophers become kings or kings take up philosophy, so that political power and wisdom coincide. Glaucon warns that many will attack him, and promises help. Socrates begins to define the philosopher: as lovers love all the young and wine-lovers any wine, the philosopher desires all of wisdom and every kind of learning. When Glaucon objects that sight-lovers and theatre-goers would then count, Socrates restricts the name to lovers of the vision of truth, who can see beauty itself and not only beautiful things; those who cannot are like dreamers.","ideas":["Political power and philosophy must meet in the same rulers","The philosopher loves the whole of wisdom, not a part","Beauty itself is one; it appears many through combination with things","Mistaking the copy for the original is like dreaming"],"terms":[{"t":"philosophos (philosopher)","d":"A lover of the whole of wisdom, who can behold the beautiful itself."},{"t":"philotheamōn (lover of sights)","d":"One who delights in beautiful sounds and sights but cannot see absolute beauty."}]},{"i":698,"j":713,"loc":"476e–479e","title":"Knowledge, opinion and their objects","summary":"To persuade the sight-lover without offending him, Socrates argues that knowledge is of what is, ignorance of what is not, and that anything which both is and is not would lie between. Defining faculties by their sphere and result, he and Glaucon agree that knowledge and opinion are distinct faculties, so opinion has a different object: neither being nor not-being, but something intermediate, darker than knowledge and clearer than ignorance. The many beautiful and just things all also appear ugly and unjust, double and half, like a riddle. Those who see only these have opinion, while those who see what is eternal and unchanging have knowledge and alone deserve the name of philosophers.","ideas":["Faculties are distinguished by what they are set over and what they achieve","Opinion is intermediate between knowledge and ignorance","The many sensible things roll between being and not-being","Philosophers love what is; others are lovers of opinion"],"terms":[{"t":"epistēmē (knowledge)","d":"The infallible faculty set over what fully is."},{"t":"doxa (opinion)","d":"The fallible faculty set over what is between being and not-being."}]},{"i":714,"j":729,"loc":"480a–486e","title":"The philosophic nature fit to rule","summary":"Socrates tells Glaucon that, since philosophers alone grasp the eternal and unchanging, they should be the guardians, for those without a clear pattern of true being in their souls are like the blind. He then lists the qualities of the philosophic nature: love of all true being, truthfulness, temperance, freedom from meanness, courage before death, justice, quickness to learn, good memory and a well-proportioned mind. Glaucon agrees that these qualities belong together. Socrates concludes that the State should be entrusted only to such people, once perfected by years and education.","ideas":["Rulers must be able to look to the true pattern of justice and beauty, as a painter looks to his model.","A soul whose desire flows toward knowledge cares little for bodily pleasure or money.","The philosophic virtues form a single set, all needed for full participation in being."],"terms":[{"t":"phusis philosophos (philosophic nature)","d":"The inborn set of traits that fits a person for philosophy and so for ruling."},{"t":"aletheia (truth)","d":"What the philosopher loves above all and finds most akin to wisdom."}]},{"i":730,"j":748,"loc":"487a–490d","title":"Adeimantus' objection and the ship of state","summary":"Adeimantus objects that Socrates wins each step of the argument, yet in practice most who pursue philosophy into later life become rogues, and the best become useless. Socrates agrees and answers with a parable: a ship whose captain is strong but half-deaf and short-sighted, whose crew quarrel over the helm, drug the captain and mock the true navigator as a stargazer. The useless philosopher is to be blamed on those who will not use him, not on himself. Socrates then restates that the true lover of knowledge strives after being and hates falsehood, and turns to ask why most philosophic natures are corrupted.","ideas":["The uselessness of good philosophers is the fault of cities that will not use them.","Just as the sick go to a doctor, the governed should seek out whoever can govern.","Philosophy's worst damage comes from its own false followers."],"terms":[{"t":"the ship of state","d":"Socrates' parable of a mutinous crew who scorn the navigator's knowledge of stars and seasons."},{"t":"kubernētēs (pilot, helmsman)","d":"The one with real knowledge of steering, standing for the true ruler."}]},{"i":749,"j":779,"loc":"490e–496e","title":"How the philosophic nature is corrupted","summary":"Socrates explains why rare philosophic natures are so often ruined. Their own virtues and the ordinary goods of beauty, wealth and rank pull them away, and, like strong seeds in bad soil, the best natures turn worst when badly raised. The real Sophist is the public itself, whose uproar in assemblies and theatres shapes the young, while hired Sophists only learn to humour a great beast. Gifted youths are flattered and steered into politics, and unworthy men then take over philosophy and bring her into disrepute. Only a small remnant stays true, sheltering like a man behind a wall in a storm.","ideas":["Great natures, badly educated, produce the greatest evils as well as the greatest goods.","The many cannot believe in absolute beauty, so they will always censure philosophers.","The Sophists' wisdom is only skill at reading the moods of the multitude, the great beast."],"terms":[{"t":"sophistēs (Sophist)","d":"A paid teacher; Socrates says the public is the greatest Sophist of all."},{"t":"the great beast","d":"Image of the crowd, whose likes and dislikes the Sophists call good and evil."}]},{"i":780,"j":808,"loc":"497a–502d","title":"Philosophy in a fitting state is possible","summary":"Socrates says no existing constitution suits the philosophic nature; only a state like theirs would let it flourish, though a living authority must preserve its spirit. Cities now take up philosophy wrongly, as a pastime for the young, whereas it should grow with age. He insists the many would accept philosophers if they saw them truly: such a person, gazing at the ordered realm of being, becomes orderly himself and would sketch a constitution like a painter working from a divine pattern on a cleaned slate. Since a king's son born a philosopher is not impossible, the ideal city is hard but not impossible.","ideas":["The philosopher imitates the eternal order he contemplates and could shape a city after it.","The founders must wipe the slate clean before drawing a constitution.","One philosopher-ruler with an obedient city would be enough to realize the plan."],"terms":[{"t":"paradeigma (pattern)","d":"The heavenly model the philosopher-painter looks to when ordering a city."},{"t":"dialektikē (dialectic)","d":"The hardest part of philosophy, which present students abandon."}]},{"i":809,"j":829,"loc":"502e–506e","title":"The Good as the greatest study","summary":"Socrates now states openly that the perfect guardian must be a philosopher, and that such natures are rare, since quickness and steadiness seldom go together. Rulers must be tested in studies to see if they can bear the highest knowledge, which is the idea of the Good, by which all else becomes useful. He notes that most say the good is pleasure and the subtler say knowledge, yet both views fall into difficulty. Everyone seeks the real good, not its appearance, so guardians cannot be ignorant of it. Pressed by Adeimantus and Glaucon for his own view, he declines to describe the Good itself but offers to speak of its offspring.","ideas":["The account of the virtues in Book IV was only an outline; a longer road is needed.","Without knowledge of the Good, all other knowledge or possession is worthless.","People accept seeming justice, but no one is satisfied with merely seeming good."],"terms":[{"t":"hē tou agathou idea (the idea of the Good)","d":"The highest object of study, which makes just and other things useful and beneficial."},{"t":"makrotera periodos (the longer way)","d":"The fuller course of study needed to see the virtues and the Good clearly."}]},{"i":830,"j":841,"loc":"507a–509b","title":"The analogy of the sun","summary":"Socrates reminds Glaucon of the distinction between the many beautiful things, which are seen, and beauty itself, which is known but not seen. Sight, unlike the other senses, needs a third thing, light, whose source is the sun. The sun is the child of the Good: as the sun gives the eye its power to see and things their visibility, the Good gives truth to what is known and the power of knowing to the knower. Truth and knowledge are like the Good but not the Good. Further, as the sun causes growth, the Good gives things their very being, while itself surpassing being in dignity and power.","ideas":["The soul fixed on what truth and being illuminate understands; turned to becoming it has only opinion.","Knowledge and truth are beautiful, but the Good is more beautiful still.","The Good is beyond being, as the sun is not itself generation."],"terms":[{"t":"the sun analogy","d":"The comparison of the sun's role in seeing with the Good's role in knowing."},{"t":"ousia (being, essence)","d":"What the Good gives to known things while itself exceeding it."}]},{"i":842,"j":853,"loc":"509c–511d","title":"The divided line","summary":"Socrates asks Glaucon to picture a line cut into the visible and the intelligible, each part cut again in the same ratio. The lowest segment holds images such as shadows and reflections; the next, the animals and made things they copy. In the intelligible part, the lower segment uses visible things as images and reasons from unexamined hypotheses down to conclusions, as in geometry; the higher rises through hypotheses to an unhypothetical first principle, using forms alone. Glaucon restates this, and Socrates assigns four states of the soul: reason, understanding, belief and conjecture, clear in proportion to the truth of their objects.","ideas":["Copy is to original as opinion is to knowledge.","Mathematicians draw figures but think about the square itself.","Dialectic treats hypotheses as stepping-stones to the first principle of all."],"terms":[{"t":"noēsis (reason)","d":"The highest state, which uses only forms and reaches a first principle."},{"t":"dianoia (understanding)","d":"Thought that reasons from hypotheses, as in geometry, between opinion and reason."},{"t":"eikasia (conjecture)","d":"Perception of shadows and images, the lowest state."}]},{"i":854,"j":870,"loc":"511e–517b","title":"The Allegory of the Cave","summary":"Socrates describes prisoners chained in an underground den since childhood, seeing only shadows cast on a wall by objects carried before a fire behind them, and taking these shadows for reality. One prisoner is freed and forced to turn toward the fire, which pains and confuses him, then dragged up into sunlight, where he slowly learns to see reflections, things, the night sky and finally the sun itself, which he grasps as cause of the seasons and all he once saw. He would pity his fellows and scorn their honours for reading shadows. Going back down, blinded by darkness, he would be mocked, and they would kill anyone who tried to free them.","ideas":["People in their ordinary state take shadows of artefacts for the truth.","Turning toward reality is painful and must be gradual.","The one who returns appears ridiculous and is in danger from those below."],"terms":[{"t":"the Cave","d":"The image of human beings bound to shadows and the ascent out to sunlight."},{"t":"periagōgē (turning around)","d":"The forced turning of the prisoner from shadows toward the light."}]},{"i":871,"j":890,"loc":"517c–521b","title":"Interpreting the Cave: education and return","summary":"Socrates links the Cave to the earlier images: the prison is the visible world, the fire the sun, the ascent the soul's rise to the intelligible, where the Good is seen last. Those who have seen it are clumsy in law courts about shadows of justice. Education is not putting sight into blind eyes but turning the whole soul toward being, a power that clever rogues use wrongly. The founders must compel the best to ascend and then return to rule. Glaucon fears this is unjust; Socrates replies that the law aims at the whole city's good, and those educated by it owe this service, ruling reluctantly.","ideas":["The capacity to learn is already in the soul; it must be turned, not implanted.","The city whose rulers least want to rule is governed best.","Only those who have a better life than politics should be compelled to rule."],"terms":[{"t":"paideia (education)","d":"The art of turning the soul from becoming to being."},{"t":"anankē (compulsion)","d":"The necessity laid on philosophers to go back down into the den."}]},{"i":891,"j":916,"loc":"521c–526c","title":"Arithmetic as the first study","summary":"Socrates and Glaucon seek studies that draw the soul from becoming to being and are also useful in war. Gymnastic and music are ruled out, as are the crafts, leaving number and calculation, which every art uses. Socrates distinguishes perceptions that invite thought, where sense reports opposites at once, as a finger seeming both large and small, from those that do not. Such contradictions make the soul ask what unity, great and small really are. Arithmetic, studied for its own sake and not for trade, forces reasoning about pure numbers, sharpens the mind and leads toward truth, so the best natures must learn it.","ideas":["Perceptions that present opposites together summon the intellect.","Unity seen as both one and many prompts the question what the one itself is.","Arithmetic has a double use, military and philosophical."],"terms":[{"t":"logistikē and arithmētikē (calculation and arithmetic)","d":"The study of number, the first discipline that converts the soul."},{"t":"parakletika (summoners)","d":"Sense experiences that call on thought by appearing contrary."}]},{"i":917,"j":943,"loc":"526d–531e","title":"Geometry, astronomy and harmonics","summary":"Glaucon praises geometry for its military uses, but Socrates values its higher part, which concerns what always is and draws the soul upward. Glaucon next proposes astronomy for its use in the seasons; Socrates corrects the order to insert solid geometry, which he says is neglected for lack of state support. He rejects stargazing as true upward looking: the visible heavens are only a beautiful pattern, and astronomy must be pursued through problems, as geometry is. Harmonics, its sister science, should likewise seek the numbers of concord rather than trust the ear. All these studies matter only when their kinship is seen, and they are only a prelude.","ideas":["Geometry concerns the eternal, despite geometers' practical language.","Real astronomy studies true motions grasped by reason, not the visible heavens.","The mathematical studies are a prelude to dialectic, not its substitute."],"terms":[{"t":"stereometria (solid geometry)","d":"The study of three dimensions, placed between plane geometry and astronomy."},{"t":"harmonia (harmonics)","d":"Study of concord, to be pursued through numbers rather than the ear."}]},{"i":944,"j":958,"loc":"532a–534e","title":"Dialectic, the coping-stone","summary":"Socrates calls dialectic the main strain to which the sciences were a prelude. By reason alone, without the senses, the dialectician moves toward each thing's essence and finally to the Good, as the freed prisoner at last saw the sun. When Glaucon asks for dialectic's nature and paths, Socrates says he could not follow without the previous studies. Mathematics only dreams of being while its hypotheses remain unexamined; dialectic alone does away with hypotheses and goes to the first principle. He restates the four divisions in proportion, and says one who cannot define the Good and defend it against objections knows only a shadow.","ideas":["Only dialectic can give an account of what each thing is.","The sciences are better called understanding than knowledge.","Dialectic is the coping-stone set over all other studies."],"terms":[{"t":"dialektikē (dialectic)","d":"The method that removes hypotheses and ascends to the first principle."},{"t":"logos (account)","d":"The ability to give and receive a reason, which marks the dialectician."}]},{"i":959,"j":989,"loc":"535a–541a","title":"Selecting rulers and the course of studies","summary":"Socrates lists the traits of those chosen for this education: steadfastness, courage, quickness, memory and love of labour, with no half-hearted industry or tolerance of involuntary ignorance. Studies begin in childhood as play, since forced learning does not stay. Selections follow at twenty and thirty. Dialectic is delayed because young arguers, like a changeling who finds his parents false, lose respect for inherited principles and become lawless. After five years of dialectic, fifteen years of public service, then at fifty they see the Good and take turns ruling. Socrates adds that women are included, and the city could start by sending all over ten to the country.","ideas":["Knowledge acquired under compulsion has no hold on the mind.","Early dialectic breeds contentiousness and contempt for law.","The ability to see studies as a connected whole marks the dialectical nature."],"terms":[{"t":"nothos (bastard)","d":"Socrates' term for a pretender to philosophy lacking a true vocation."},{"t":"eristic","d":"Arguing for victory and amusement, contrasted with dialectic."}]},{"i":990,"j":1008,"loc":"541b–546c","title":"Returning to the four corrupt constitutions","summary":"Socrates recalls the features of the perfect city: common wives and children, common education, and philosopher-kings over propertyless guardians. Glaucon reminds him of the point, at the start of Book V, when he was about to list four flawed constitutions. Socrates names them: the Cretan and Spartan type, oligarchy, democracy and tyranny. Since states grow out of human characters, there are five types of individual too. He sets the plan of examining each city and its matching man, in order to compare the happiness of the most just and most unjust, and asks how timocracy first arises from the best city.","ideas":["Constitutions vary as human characters vary.","The order of decline is timocracy, oligarchy, democracy, tyranny.","The study aims to settle whether justice or injustice brings happiness."],"terms":[{"t":"timokratia (timocracy)","d":"Rule by the love of honour, the first decline from the best city."},{"t":"aristokratia (aristocracy)","d":"Rule of the best, the city just described."}]},{"i":1009,"j":1029,"loc":"546d–550d","title":"The Muses' number and the timocratic man","summary":"Socrates has the Muses explain, playfully, that even the best city must decay: rulers will miscalculate a complex number governing good births, unfit children will rule, music will be neglected, and metals will be mixed. Discord follows; the iron and bronze turn to money-making, and the classes divide the land and enslave the producers. The new timocracy keeps common meals and military training but fears philosophers, honours war and hides gold. Adeimantus then hears of the timocratic man: ambitious, fond of hunting, rough with slaves. He arises as the son of a retiring father, provoked by his mother and servants into love of honour.","ideas":["Everything that comes into being must also pass away.","Timocracy mixes good and bad but is dominated by spirit and ambition.","The son is pulled between the father's reason and others' passions, and settles on spirit."],"terms":[{"t":"the nuptial number","d":"The obscure number governing births whose neglect starts the city's decline."},{"t":"thumoeides (the spirited part)","d":"The element of soul that rules in timocracy."}]},{"i":1030,"j":1052,"loc":"550e–555b","title":"Oligarchy and the oligarchic man","summary":"Socrates defines oligarchy as rule based on a property qualification. It arises as private gold accumulates and wealth is honoured above virtue. Its defects are many: rulers chosen by money, like pilots chosen by wealth; a city split into rich and poor; inability to wage war; one man doing many jobs; and ruined citizens left as drones, some becoming criminals. The oligarchic man is the son of a timocrat whose father was ruined; frightened, he enthrones appetite for money, making reason and spirit its servants. He is thrifty and respectable, holding his drone-like desires down by fear rather than reason, a divided man.","ideas":["Wealth and virtue rise and fall against one another like scales.","The oligarchic city is really two cities at war.","Enforced respectability is not the harmony of a soul in true virtue."],"terms":[{"t":"oligarchia (oligarchy)","d":"Rule by the wealthy few, fixed by a property qualification."},{"t":"kēphēn (drone)","d":"An idle consumer of the city; the stinging kind become criminals."}]},{"i":1053,"j":1068,"loc":"555c–558c","title":"The rise and character of democracy","summary":"Socrates explains that oligarchy's insatiable pursuit of wealth leads rulers to let spendthrift youths ruin themselves, multiplying impoverished, armed malcontents. Lenders keep inflicting usury, and the ruling young grow soft. When rich and poor meet in danger, the hardy poor see the rich as weak. A small occasion brings on civil war; the poor win and establish democracy, with office often given by lot. Democracy is full of freedom and frankness, allowing every kind of life, a many-coloured robe and a bazaar of constitutions. It ignores the city's founding principles, tolerates the condemned, and gives a sort of equality to equals and unequals.","ideas":["Love of wealth and moderation cannot thrive together in one city.","Democracy is born when the poor win and share office equally.","Freedom makes democracy varied and attractive but disorderly."],"terms":[{"t":"dēmokratia (democracy)","d":"Rule by the people, born when the poor overthrow the rich."},{"t":"eleutheria (freedom)","d":"The good that democracy prizes and treats as limitless."}]},{"i":1069,"j":1086,"loc":"558d–562a","title":"Necessary desires and the democratic man","summary":"To describe the democratic man, Socrates first distinguishes necessary desires, which cannot be got rid of and benefit us, from unnecessary ones, which can be trained away and do harm; the first make money and the second spend it. The son of an oligarch tastes drones' honey, and outside allies inflame his unnecessary desires. After struggles, false conceits seize the citadel of his soul, expel modesty and temperance under insulting names, and welcome insolence and anarchy as breeding and liberty. Later he may settle into treating all pleasures as equal, following whichever comes, now drinking, now dieting, now philosophizing, without law or order.","ideas":["Necessary desires serve health and life; unnecessary ones exceed them.","The democratic soul renames vices as virtues.","The democratic man treats all pleasures as equal and lives by the mood of the day."],"terms":[{"t":"anankaiai epithumiai (necessary desires)","d":"Desires we cannot remove and whose satisfaction benefits us."},{"t":"isonomia (equality)","d":"The democratic man's rule of giving every desire its turn."}]},{"i":1087,"j":1108,"loc":"562b–566c","title":"From democracy to tyranny","summary":"Socrates argues that, as excessive wealth destroyed oligarchy, insatiable desire for freedom destroys democracy. Drunk on freedom, the city punishes moderate rulers, and anarchy spreads to families, schools, slaves, women and even animals, until citizens bear no authority, not even law. Extreme liberty turns into extreme slavery. The drones, now dominant, feed on the rich, who are driven to become oligarchs in fact. The people raise up a champion, and this protector, tasting kindred blood through false accusations like the Arcadian wolf-man, asks for a bodyguard and becomes a tyrant, standing in the chariot of state.","ideas":["Excess of anything tends to provoke a reaction into its opposite.","Tyranny grows from the root of a popular protector.","Democracy is divided into drones, the wealthy, and the working people."],"terms":[{"t":"prostatēs (protector, champion)","d":"The people's leader who turns into a tyrant."},{"t":"turannis (tyranny)","d":"Rule of one man by force, the fourth and worst constitution."}]},{"i":1109,"j":1122,"loc":"566d–569b","title":"The tyrant's rule and the poets","summary":"Socrates traces the tyrant's career. At first he smiles at all, cancels debts and divides land; then he stirs up wars to keep the people needing a leader and poor from taxes. When the brave, wise and wealthy object, he purges them, the reverse of a doctor's purge, and relies on mercenaries and freed slaves. Socrates remarks that the tragic poets praise tyranny, so they are excluded from the city. Paying his army, the tyrant seizes temple treasures and then the people's wealth; if the people resist, he uses force on them like a son beating his father. Liberty has fallen into the bitterest slavery.","ideas":["The tyrant must remove the best people and live among the worst.","Tragic poets praise tyranny and are honoured by tyrants and democracies.","The people fleeing the smoke of slavery fall into the fire of tyranny."],"terms":[{"t":"katharmos (purgation)","d":"The tyrant's removal of the best citizens, inverting medical purging."},{"t":"doruphoroi (bodyguard)","d":"The armed guard the tyrant requests and enlarges."}]},{"i":1123,"j":1151,"loc":"569c–576b","title":"Lawless desires and the tyrannical man","summary":"Socrates begins Book IX by examining appetites again: some unnecessary desires are lawless, and in everyone they stir in dreams, when reason sleeps, prompting even incest or murder. A man who calms appetite and spirit and wakes reason before sleep is least troubled. The tyrannical man is the democrat's son, whose seducers plant in him a master passion, Eros, like a great winged drone, guarded by madness. His desires consume his property, then his parents', then lead to theft and temple robbery; what he once did only in dreams he does awake. Such men, if many, make the worst of them tyrant.","ideas":["A lawless wild beast lies in everyone and appears in sleep.","Eros as master passion makes the soul a tyranny.","The tyrannical man is a waking version of the dream."],"terms":[{"t":"paranomoi epithumiai (lawless desires)","d":"Unnecessary appetites that break every law, seen in dreams."},{"t":"erōs turannos (Love as tyrant)","d":"The ruling passion that governs the tyrannical soul."}]},{"i":1152,"j":1172,"loc":"576c–580c","title":"The tyrant is the most miserable: first proof","summary":"Glaucon takes over as respondent. Socrates asks him to judge happiness not by the tyrant's pomp but as one who knows his private life. The tyrannized city is enslaved, poor, full of fear and grief; so too is the tyrannical soul, whose best parts are enslaved to a mad ruling part. Socrates adds that the most miserable is the tyrannical man who actually becomes a tyrant, comparing him to a slave-owner carried into the wilderness among his slaves and hostile neighbours. Glaucon then ranks the five types, and the result is proclaimed: the most just is happiest and most kingly, the most unjust most wretched.","ideas":["The individual soul mirrors the condition of the city of its type.","The real tyrant is the real slave, never free or a friend.","Happiness follows the ranking: kingly, timocratic, oligarchic, democratic, tyrannical."],"terms":[{"t":"basilikos (kingly)","d":"The just person who rules himself, ranked happiest."},{"t":"the parallel of city and soul","d":"The method of reading the individual from the matching constitution."}]},{"i":1173,"j":1183,"loc":"580d–582d","title":"Second proof: the three lives and their pleasures","summary":"Socrates offers a second proof from the three-part soul. Each part has its pleasure: the learning part loves wisdom, the spirited loves honour, the appetitive loves gain or money. So there are three kinds of people, each praising his own life as pleasantest. The judge must be experience, wisdom and reason. The philosopher has tasted the other pleasures, while the others have not tasted learning; he alone has wisdom and reason, the instruments of judging. Therefore the philosopher's pleasure is pleasantest, the honour-lover's second, the money-lover's last.","ideas":["Each part of the soul has its own desire and pleasure.","Only the philosopher has experience of all three pleasures.","Reason, the instrument of judgment, belongs to the philosopher."],"terms":[{"t":"philosophos, philotimos, philokerdēs","d":"Lover of wisdom, lover of honour, lover of gain: the three types of life."},{"t":"empeiria (experience)","d":"One of the criteria, with wisdom and reason, for judging pleasures."}]},{"i":1184,"j":1210,"loc":"582e–588a","title":"Third proof: true and illusory pleasure","summary":"Socrates gives a third proof: only the wise man's pleasure is wholly true. He distinguishes pleasure, pain and an intermediate rest; the sick and suffering mistake relief from pain for pleasure, which is only appearance, like thinking the middle region is the top. Some pleasures, such as smell, have no prior pain. Filling the soul with knowledge is truer than filling the body with food, since its objects have more being. Those who live for appetite or unreasoning honour graze like cattle and fight over shadows of pleasure, as over Helen's phantom. Reason-led parts get their truest pleasures; by calculation the king lives 729 times more pleasantly than the tyrant.","ideas":["Relief from pain is not genuine pleasure but an illusion of contrast.","Pleasure is truer when one is filled with what has more real being.","When the whole soul follows reason each part enjoys its own best pleasure."],"terms":[{"t":"hēsuchia (neutral rest)","d":"The state between pleasure and pain that people mistake for either."},{"t":"plērōsis (filling)","d":"Pleasure as replenishment, truer when what fills is more real."}]},{"i":1211,"j":1230,"loc":"588b–592a","title":"The image of the soul and the city within","summary":"Socrates returns to the claim that injustice profits one who seems just. He fashions an image of the soul: a many-headed beast, a lion and a man, joined and covered in a human shape. The defender of injustice is really advising one to feed the beast and lion and starve the man; the just person makes the man master, tending the beast like a farmer with the lion as ally. Vices are explained as disorders among these, and it is best to be ruled by the divine within, or else from outside. The wise man orders his life by the city within, which Glaucon notes exists nowhere on earth; Socrates says its pattern is laid up in heaven.","ideas":["Injustice enslaves the best part of oneself to the worst.","Punishment tames the beast in the soul and so benefits the offender.","The ideal city serves as a pattern for ordering one's own soul."],"terms":[{"t":"the many-headed beast","d":"The appetitive part, pictured as a monster with tame and wild heads."},{"t":"paradeigma en ouranōi (pattern in heaven)","d":"The model city one may look to and live by, whether or not it exists."}]},{"i":1231,"j":1245,"loc":"592b–597e","title":"Imitation and the three beds","summary":"Socrates returns to poetry, saying the exclusion of imitative poetry now seems even more right, since it harms the minds of hearers who lack knowledge of its nature. Though he loves Homer, truth comes first. He asks what imitation is. There is one form for each set of things sharing a name, such as bed. God makes the single bed in nature, the carpenter makes a particular bed, and the painter makes an image of a bed. The painter, and likewise the tragic poet, is an imitator third from the king and the truth, copying things not as they are but as they appear.","ideas":["God makes only one ideal bed, since two would imply a third behind them.","The craftsman makes not the form but a particular semblance of it.","Painting imitates appearance, not reality."],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"Making images of things as they appear, three removes from truth."},{"t":"eidos (form)","d":"The single nature shared by all things with a common name."}]},{"i":1246,"j":1266,"loc":"598a–602a","title":"Homer's lack of knowledge","summary":"Socrates argues that an imitator can seem to do everything because he touches only a small, image-like part. Those who claim Homer and the tragedians know all arts and virtue may have been deceived. One who could make originals would not devote himself to images. Homer founded no well-governed city, won no war, invented nothing, and left no way of life like Pythagoras; if he could truly educate, followers would never have let him go. Socrates distinguishes three arts for each thing, using, making and imitating: the user knows, the maker has right belief from him, and the imitator has neither.","ideas":["Poets copy images of virtue without grasping the truth.","Poetry stripped of metre and music looks poor.","The user has knowledge, the maker right opinion, the imitator neither."],"terms":[{"t":"chrēsis, poiēsis, mimēsis (use, making, imitation)","d":"Three arts concerned with each thing, ranked by knowledge."},{"t":"orthē doxa (right opinion)","d":"What the maker gets by learning from the user who knows."}]},{"i":1267,"j":1295,"loc":"602b–607e","title":"Poetry's appeal to the lower soul","summary":"Socrates asks which part of the soul imitation addresses. Optical illusions are corrected by measuring, the work of reason, so what contradicts measure belongs to an inferior part, and imitation befriends that part. In poetry, a good man in grief resists lamentation through reason and law, but the restless, grieving part is easier to imitate and pleases crowds. The heaviest charge is that poetry harms even the best: in the theatre we enjoy indulging pity and laughter we would be ashamed of at home, and this feeds the passions. Only hymns to gods and praises of good men are admitted; poetry may return if she or her defenders prove her useful.","ideas":["Measuring and calculation rescue us from appearances.","Poetry nourishes the passions that reason should govern.","There is an ancient quarrel between philosophy and poetry."],"terms":[{"t":"to logistikon (the calculating part)","d":"The rational element that trusts measure and resists grief."},{"t":"the ancient quarrel","d":"Socrates' name for the long hostility between philosophy and poetry."}]},{"i":1296,"j":1314,"loc":"608a–611d","title":"The immortality of the soul","summary":"Socrates says the greatest rewards of virtue remain, since an immortal soul should think of the whole of time; Glaucon is astonished at the claim. Socrates argues that each thing is destroyed only by its own inherent evil, as eyes by disease or iron by rust. The soul's evils, injustice and the like, do not kill it, and a bodily evil cannot destroy the soul unless it makes the soul more unjust. So the soul exists forever, and the number of souls stays fixed. To see the soul's real nature, one must look past its present state, encrusted like the sea-god Glaucus, to its love of wisdom and kinship with the divine.","ideas":["Only a thing's own peculiar evil can destroy it.","Injustice does not kill the soul; it keeps the unjust very much alive.","The soul as seen now is marred by its union with the body."],"terms":[{"t":"athanasia (immortality)","d":"The soul's indestructibility, argued from its inherent evil."},{"t":"Glaucus","d":"A sea-god whose shape is hidden by shells and weed, an image for the soul."}]},{"i":1315,"j":1323,"loc":"611e–613c","title":"Restoring the rewards of justice","summary":"Socrates notes that justice has now been shown best in itself, apart from rewards, like having Gyges' ring. He asks Glaucon to return what was borrowed for the argument, the supposition that the just might seem unjust. The gods know the just and the unjust, so the just are friends of the gods, and even seeming misfortunes will work for their good. Among men, the unjust are like runners who start fast but end crownless, while the just win the prize. Socrates reclaims for the just all the blessings Glaucon had assigned to the successful unjust, and announces still greater rewards after death.","ideas":["Justice has been shown best for the soul before considering rewards.","The gods care for one who strives to become like God through virtue.","Over a whole life the unjust are found out and the just win honour."],"terms":[{"t":"the ring of Gyges","d":"The invisibility ring of Book II, invoked as no longer relevant to justice's worth."},{"t":"homoiōsis theōi (becoming like God)","d":"Pursuit of virtue so far as humans can attain likeness to the divine."}]},{"i":1324,"j":1339,"loc":"613d–616d","title":"The Myth of Er: judgment and the thousand-year journey","summary":"Socrates tells the tale of Er, a Pamphylian killed in battle, whose body did not decay and who revived on the pyre to report the other world. Souls came to judges between two openings in the earth and two in the heavens; the just went up to the right, the unjust down to the left, wearing tokens of their deeds. In a meadow, returning souls exchanged tales: those from below wept over a thousand years of suffering, those from above described heavenly sights. Each wrong was repaid tenfold. Er heard that the tyrant Ardiaeus and other incurables were refused exit by the roaring mouth and dragged away by fiery men.","ideas":["Er is appointed messenger of the afterlife to humankind.","Rewards and penalties are ten times the deeds, over a thousand years.","The incurably wicked, mostly tyrants, are never released."],"terms":[{"t":"the Myth of Er","d":"Socrates' closing tale of judgment, reward and rebirth of souls."},{"t":"Ardiaeus","d":"A tyrant who murdered his father and brother, held below forever."}]},{"i":1340,"j":1354,"loc":"616e–619d","title":"The spindle of Necessity and the lots of lives","summary":"After seven days the souls journey to a column of light binding the heavens, from which hangs the spindle of Necessity with eight nested whorls of differing sizes, colours and speeds, each bearing a siren singing one note. Around sit the three Fates, Lachesis, Clotho and Atropos, singing of past, present and future. A prophet takes lots and patterns of lives from Lachesis and proclaims that each soul chooses its own guardian spirit and life, and the blame lies with the chooser, not God. Socrates breaks in to urge that the supreme task is learning to tell the better life from the worse, choosing the mean.","ideas":["The cosmos is pictured as a spindle turning on Necessity's knees.","Virtue is free, and responsibility belongs to the one who chooses.","Philosophy's purpose is to choose the life that makes the soul more just."],"terms":[{"t":"Anankē (Necessity)","d":"The goddess on whose knees the cosmic spindle turns."},{"t":"daimōn (guardian spirit)","d":"The genius each soul chooses, which guards and fulfils its chosen life."}]},{"i":1355,"j":1364,"loc":"619e–621d","title":"The choice of lives and the river of forgetfulness","summary":"The first chooser, a soul from heaven whose virtue was mere habit, seizes the greatest tyranny and then laments, blaming everything but himself; those from below, taught by suffering, choose more carefully. Er sees Orpheus choose a swan, Ajax a lion, Agamemnon an eagle, Atalanta an athlete, Thersites a monkey, and Odysseus, freed from ambition, the quiet life of a private man. The Fates ratify each choice, the souls drink from the river of Unmindfulness, forget, and shoot like stars to birth, while Er wakes on the pyre. Socrates closes by urging that we hold to justice, believing the soul immortal.","ideas":["Habitual virtue without philosophy can fail at the moment of choice.","Choices often follow the experiences of a previous life.","The tale, if heeded, will carry us safely past forgetfulness."],"terms":[{"t":"Lēthē (Forgetfulness)","d":"The barren plain whose river makes souls forget before rebirth."},{"t":"Ameles (Unmindfulness)","d":"The river whose water no vessel holds, drunk by all souls."}]}]});